<body>

<!-- ─── HEADER ─── -->
<header class="report-header">
    <h1>Biomedical Research Landscape 2024–2026</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">Publication Trends, Topic Dynamics, and Open Access Patterns — A trend analysis of {total_pubs:,} biomedical research abstracts indexed in PubMed</p>
    <p class="meta">Data source: NCBI PubMed &middot; Coverage: January 2024 – March 2026 &middot; Report date: May 2026</p>
</header>

<div class="container">

<!-- ─── KPI CARDS ─── -->
<div class="kpi-row">
    <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="metric">{total_pubs:,}</div>
        <div class="metric-label">Total Publications</div>
        <div class="metric-detail">{data['pubs_by_year']['2024']:,} in 2024 &middot; {data['pubs_by_year']['2025']:,} in 2025 &middot; {data['pubs_by_year']['2026']:,} in 2026</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card" style="border-top-color: var(--accent);">
        <div class="metric" style="color: var(--accent);">{oa_rate}%</div>
        <div class="metric-label">Open Access Rate</div>
        <div class="metric-detail">{data['oa_rate_by_year']['2024']}% (2024) &middot; {data['oa_rate_by_year']['2025']}% (2025) &middot; {data['oa_rate_by_year']['2026']}% (2026)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card" style="border-top-color: #7c3aed;">
        <div class="metric" style="color: #7c3aed;">{median_auth:.0f}</div>
        <div class="metric-label">Median Authors per Publication</div>
        <div class="metric-detail">Consistent across all three years</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card" style="border-top-color: #0d9488;">
        <div class="metric" style="color: #0d9488;">{top10_share}%</div>
        <div class="metric-label">Top 10 Countries Share</div>
        <div class="metric-detail">Of publications with known country affiliation</div>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ─── SECTION 1: Publication Volume ─── -->
<section id="publication-volume-trends">
    <h2><span class="section-num">1</span> Publication Volume & Temporal Distribution</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">
        This section examines the monthly and yearly distribution of publications, revealing the natural skew toward 2024 due to PubMed indexing lag. As articles age, they accumulate more complete indexing entries, making 2024 appear dominant in raw count.
        <span class="data-note">Indexing Lag</span>
    </p>

    <div class="card">
        <div class="callout warn">
            <strong>Temporal skew is by design.</strong> Approximately {round(data['pubs_by_year']['2024']/total_pubs*100,1)}% of records are from 2024, reflecting real-world PubMed indexing patterns where older publications accumulate more indexed entries over time. 2025 and 2026 have progressively fewer records. Year-over-year comparisons should use rates or normalized metrics rather than raw counts.
        </div>
        <div class="chart-container">
            {vol_chart}
        </div>
        <p style="font-size:13px;color:var(--text-muted);margin-top:12px;">
            The January 2024 spike ({data['monthly_volume']['2024-01']:,} publications) likely reflects batch-indexed backfill rather than a true publication surge. After February 2024, volumes stabilize to a more representative monthly baseline of 30–100 publications, gradually declining as indexing lag increases for more recent months. The uptick in early 2026 (51–98/month) may reflect active indexing catch-up cycles.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="two-col">
        <div class="card">
            <div class="chart-container">{year_chart}</div>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
            <h3 style="font-size:14px;margin-bottom:12px;color:var(--text);">Year-over-Year Summary</h3>
            <table>
                <tr><th>Year</th><th>Publications</th><th>Share</th></tr>
                <tr><td>2024</td><td>{data['pubs_by_year']['2024']:,}</td><td>{round(data['pubs_by_year']['2024']/total_pubs*100,1)}%</td></tr>
                <tr><td>2025</td><td>{data['pubs_by_year']['2025']:,}</td><td>{round(data['pubs_by_year']['2025']/total_pubs*100,1)}%</td></tr>
                <tr><td>2026</td><td>{data['pubs_by_year']['2026']:,}</td><td>{round(data['pubs_by_year']['2026']/total_pubs*100,1)}%</td></tr>
            </table>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── SECTION 2: Geographic Landscape ─── -->
<section id="geographic-landscape">
    <h2><span class="section-num">2</span> Geographic Landscape of Biomedical Research</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">
        Identifying the top contributing countries by publication count, examining how geographic contributions shift over the 2024–2026 window, and highlighting emerging or declining national research presences.
    </p>

    <div class="card">
        <div class="chart-container">{country_chart}</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
        <div class="chart-container">{country_year_chart}</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
        <h3 style="font-size:14px;margin-bottom:12px;color:var(--text);">Country Share Shifts (2024 → 2025)</h3>
        <p style="font-size:12px;color:var(--text-muted);margin-bottom:10px;">
            Share of total publications by country, comparing 2024 vs. 2025. Positive values indicate a growing share; negative indicates declining relative share. Note that 2025 has fewer total records due to indexing lag, so share shifts should be interpreted cautiously.
        </p>
        <table>
            <tr><th>Country</th><th>Share 2024</th><th>Share 2025</th><th>Change</th></tr>
            {country_change_rows}
        </table>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Key Geographic Insight:</strong> China dominates with {data['top10_countries']['China']:,} publications ({round(data['top10_countries']['China']/total_pubs*100,1)}% of total), but its share of 2025 publications ({country_shares['China']['share_2025']}%) is lower than its 2024 share ({country_shares['China']['share_2024']}%), potentially reflecting indexing lag rather than a real decline. India's share rose from {country_shares['India']['share_2024']}% to {country_shares['India']['share_2025']}%, a noteworthy signal even considering the small 2025 sample.
    </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── SECTION 3: Topic & MeSH Analysis ─── -->
<section id="topic-and-mesh-analysis">
    <h2><span class="section-num">3</span> Research Topic & MeSH Keyword Analysis</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">
        Examining the most prevalent major topics and MeSH keywords, identifying trending or emerging research areas, and noting topic shifts between years. Note that many newer publications (especially 2025–2026) lack MeSH indexing, which underrepresents their topic distribution.
    </p>

    <div class="card">
        <div class="callout warn">
            <strong>Missing MeSH data.</strong> Only {sum(data['top_topics'].values())} of {total_pubs:,} records ({round(sum(data['top_topics'].values())/total_pubs*100,1)}%) have a populated <code>major_topic</code> field. Newer publications are less likely to be MeSH-indexed, so 2025–2026 topics are underrepresented. This should be considered when interpreting growth rates.
        </div>
        <div class="chart-container">{topic_chart}</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
        <h3 style="font-size:14px;margin-bottom:12px;color:var(--text);">Topic Distribution by Year</h3>
        <table>
            <tr><th>Major Topic</th><th>2024</th><th>2025</th><th>2026</th></tr>
            {topic_year_rows}
        </table>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
        <h3 style="font-size:14px;margin-bottom:12px;color:var(--text);">Emerging Topic Growth Rate (Month-over-Month)</h3>
        <p style="font-size:12px;color:var(--text-muted);margin-bottom:10px;">
            Average month-over-month growth in publication counts for the most tracked MeSH topics. Positive values indicate acceleration; negative values indicate deceleration. Computed only for topics with sufficient monthly data points.
        </p>
        <table>
            <tr><th>Topic</th><th>Total Pubs</th><th>Avg. MoM Growth</th></tr>
            {emerging_rows}
        </table>
    </div>

    <div class="two-col">
        <div class="card">
            <h3 style="font-size:14px;margin-bottom:12px;color:var(--text);">Top 20 MeSH Keywords</h3>
            <table>
                <tr><th>#</th><th>Keyword</th><th>Count</th></tr>
                {kw_rows}
            </table>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
            <div class="callout">
                <strong>Emerging signals:</strong> <em>RNA, Long Noncoding</em> shows the highest month-over-month growth at +33.3%, though from a small base (7 publications). <em>Carcinoma, Non-Small-Cell Lung</em> also shows strong growth at +16.7%. Meanwhile, <em>COVID-19</em> — the most prevalent topic at 43 publications — shows declining momentum (−16.9% MoM), consistent with the pandemic research winding down. These trends should be validated against the full PubMed corpus as indexing catches up.
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── SECTION 4: Open Access ─── -->
<section id="open-access-patterns">
    <h2><span class="section-num">4</span> Open Access Adoption & Journal Landscape</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">
        Quantifying the proportion of open-access vs. closed-access publications, identifying leading journals by volume, and assessing whether open-access rates are changing over time or vary by country and research type.
    </p>

    <div class="card">
        <div class="chart-container">{oa_chart}</div>
        <p style="font-size:13px;color:var(--text-muted);margin-top:12px;">
            The open access rate fluctuates monthly between ~35% and ~88%, with an overall rate of {oa_rate}%. The 2025 yearly rate ({data['oa_rate_by_year']['2025']}%) exceeds 2024 ({data['oa_rate_by_year']['2024']}%), suggesting a trend toward greater OA adoption. The 2026 rate ({data['oa_rate_by_year']['2026']}%) is lower but based on very few records and is not yet representative. The high variability in early months (especially the low rates in Feb–May 2024) may reflect indexing patterns where non-OA articles are indexed first.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="two-col">
        <div class="card">
            <div class="chart-container">{oa_year_chart}</div>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
            <div class="chart-container">{oa_type_chart}</div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
        <h3 style="font-size:14px;margin-bottom:12px;color:var(--text);">Open Access Rate by Country (Top 10)</h3>
        <div class="chart-container">{oa_country_chart}</div>
        <p style="font-size:13px;color:var(--text-muted);margin-top:10px;">
            Iran leads with an {data['oa_by_country']['Iran']}% open access rate, followed by Japan ({data['oa_by_country']['Japan']}%) and the USA ({data['oa_by_country']['USA']}%). China, despite being the largest contributor by volume, has the lowest OA rate among the top 10 at {data['oa_by_country']['China']}%. This gap may reflect differences in funder mandates, journal preferences, or institutional OA infrastructure.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
        <h3 style="font-size:14px;margin-bottom:12px;color:var(--text);">Top Journals by Publication Volume</h3>
        <div class="chart-container">{journal_chart}</div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── SECTION 5: Research Type & Collaboration ─── -->
<section id="research-type-collaboration">
    <h2><span class="section-num">5</span> Research Type Distribution & Collaboration Indicators</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">
        Breaking down publications by research type, analyzing author count distributions as a proxy for collaboration intensity, and exploring how these vary by geography and topic.
    </p>

    <div class="two-col">
        <div class="card">
            <div class="chart-container" style="text-align:center;">{type_donut}</div>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
            <h3 style="font-size:14px;margin-bottom:12px;color:var(--text);">Research Type Breakdown</h3>
            <table>
                <tr><th>Research Type</th><th>Count</th><th>Share</th></tr>
                {type_rows}
            </table>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
        <h3 style="font-size:14px;margin-bottom:12px;color:var(--text);">Median Authors per Publication by Country</h3>
        <div class="chart-container">{auth_country_chart}</div>
        <p style="font-size:13px;color:var(--text-muted);margin-top:10px;">
            Italy and China/Japan show the highest median author counts (7–8), suggesting larger collaborative teams. India, Iran, South Korea, and the UK tend toward smaller teams (median 5). The overall median of {median_auth:.0f} authors per publication remains stable across all three years.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
        <h3 style="font-size:14px;margin-bottom:12px;color:var(--text);">Collaboration & Research Type Insights</h3>
        <div class="two-col">
            <div class="callout">
                <strong>Preprints have the largest teams.</strong> Preprints average a median of {data['median_authors_by_type']['Preprint']:.0f} authors, compared to {data['median_authors_by_type']['Journal Article']:.0f} for journal articles. This may reflect the prevalence of preprints in large-scale collaborative research (e.g., genomics, clinical trials).
            </div>
            <div class="callout">
                <strong>Preprints are 100% open access.</strong> All {data['research_type_dist'].get('Preprint', 0)} preprint records in the dataset are open access, compared to {data['oa_by_research_type']['Journal Article']}% for journal articles and {data['oa_by_research_type']['Review']}% for reviews. Case reports also show high OA rates at {data['oa_by_research_type']['Case Report']}%.
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── SECTION 6: Key Takeaways ─── -->
<section id="key-takeaways">
    <h2><span class="section-num">6</span> Key Takeaways & Strategic Implications</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">
        Synthesized findings with actionable insights for research policy, funding prioritization, and institutional strategy.
    </p>

    <div class="takeaway-card">
        <h4>1. Open Access is Gaining Momentum</h4>
        <p>The 2025 OA rate of {data['oa_rate_by_year']['2025']}% marks a meaningful increase from {data['oa_rate_by_year']['2024']}% in 2024, suggesting funder mandates (e.g., NIH, Plan S) are having measurable impact. However, China — the largest contributor by volume — lags at {data['oa_by_country']['China']}% OA, indicating a significant untapped area for OA policy advocacy. Funders should prioritize OA infrastructure investments in high-output, low-OA countries.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway-card">
        <h4>2. China Dominates Output, But Watch India and Iran</h4>
        <p>China accounts for {round(data['top10_countries']['China']/total_pubs*100,1)}% of all publications, but India's share of 2025 publications ({country_shares['India']['share_2025']}%) exceeds its 2024 share ({country_shares['India']['share_2024']}%), and Iran shows a similar upward trend. These countries represent growing research capacity that funding strategists should monitor for collaboration opportunities and capacity-building investments.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway-card">
        <h4>3. COVID-19 Research is Declining; Oncology and Microbiome are Steady</h4>
        <p>COVID-19 remains the top MeSH topic by total count (43 publications), but it shows the steepest month-over-month decline (−16.9% MoM). Meanwhile, Diabetes Mellitus Type 2 (24), Breast Neoplasms (17), and Gastrointestinal Microbiome (16) maintain steady presence. Funders shifting away from pandemic-response portfolios should redirect toward chronic disease and microbiome research, which show sustained demand.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway-card">
        <h4>4. Emerging Topics Signal Future Investment Areas</h4>
        <p>Long Noncoding RNA (+33.3% MoM) and Non-Small-Cell Lung Carcinoma (+16.7% MoM) are the fastest-growing topics, albeit from small bases. These represent early-stage research waves likely to accelerate. Strategic early investment in these areas — particularly in related infrastructure (sequencing, clinical trial networks) — could yield high returns as these fields mature.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway-card">
        <h4>5. Collaboration Patterns Vary Significantly by Geography</h4>
        <p>Median author counts range from 5 (India, Iran, UK, South Korea) to 8 (Italy), indicating meaningful differences in collaboration culture. Institutions seeking international partnerships should consider that larger teams may indicate more multi-site studies, while smaller teams may suggest more independent research programs. Both models have value, but funding mechanisms should be tailored accordingly.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway-card">
        <h4>6. Temporal Data Requires Careful Interpretation</h4>
        <p>The {round(data['pubs_by_year']['2024']/total_pubs*100,1)}% concentration of records in 2024 is an indexing artifact, not a research decline. Policy analysts should normalize by time period and account for indexing lag when assessing growth trends. The 2025 OA rate increase and India's share gain are promising signals, but both should be confirmed as the 2025–2026 indexing catches up.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── FOOTER ─── -->
<div class="footnote">
    <p><strong>Data Source:</strong> NCBI PubMed, via Kaggle (kanchana1990/biomedical-research-abstracts-20242026). Analysis covers {total_pubs:,} records from January 2024 through March 2026.</p>
    <p><strong>Methodology:</strong> Country names were cleaned and consolidated from raw affiliation fields (e.g., "USA", "United States", and US state abbreviations were merged). Research types were derived from PubMed publication-type tags. Open access status reflects PubMed Central (PMC) availability. Month-over-month growth rates were computed only for MeSH topics with at least 3 months of data.</p>
    <p><strong>Limitations:</strong> 1) Indexing lag causes temporal skew — 2024 is overrepresented. 2) Only {round(sum(data['top_topics'].values())/total_pubs*100,1)}% of records have populated <code>major_topic</code> fields, limiting topic analysis. 3) Country data reflects first-author affiliation only and had {456} unknown entries after cleaning. 4) The 2026 OA rate ({data['oa_rate_by_year']['2026']}%) is based on only {data['pubs_by_year']['2026']} records and is not yet representative.</p>
